AWS PrivateLink — Real-Time Explanation

The easiest way to understand AWS PrivateLink is:

AWS PrivateLink lets you privately access a service through private IP addresses, without exposing the service to the public internet.

It is especially important for Interface VPC Endpoints.

1. Real-world example first

Imagine your company has an application running in a private VPC:

Your Company VPC
────────────────────────────

Private EC2
    |
    | HTTPS
    v
 ????
    |
    v
Some Service

Your EC2 needs to access a service owned by another company.

For example:

Your VPC
   |
   | Private connection
   v
Payment Service

You don't want:

EC2
 ↓
Internet
 ↓
Public IP
 ↓
Payment Service

Instead, you want:

EC2
 ↓
Private IP
 ↓
AWS PrivateLink
 ↓
Payment Service

That's the basic idea behind PrivateLink.

2. What problem does PrivateLink solve?

Suppose you have:

VPC A
10.0.0.0/16

and another company has:

VPC B
192.168.0.0/16

You want applications in VPC A to consume a service running in VPC B.

One traditional approach could involve:

VPC A
   |
   v
VPC Peering / Transit Gateway
   |
   v
VPC B
   |
   v
Service

But now you're connecting networks.

PrivateLink takes a different approach:

VPC A
   |
   v
Interface Endpoint
   |
   v
PrivateLink
   |
   v
Service

You don't need general network connectivity between the two VPCs.

3. The most important architecture

There are two sides.

Service Provider

The company providing the service.

Service Consumer

The company/application consuming the service.

              SERVICE PROVIDER
              ────────────────
                    |
             Load Balancer
                    |
              Application
                    |
              Endpoint Service
                    |
                    |
              AWS PrivateLink
                    |
                    |
              Interface Endpoint
                    |
              ────────────────
              SERVICE CONSUMER
                    |
                  EC2

The consumer sees an Interface Endpoint ENI inside its VPC.

4. What is an Interface Endpoint?

This connects directly to your previous question.

When you create an Interface VPC Endpoint, AWS creates an Elastic Network Interface (ENI) in your subnet.

For example:

VPC
│
├── Private Subnet
│      │
│      ├── EC2
│      │
│      └── Endpoint ENI
│             Private IP:
│             10.0.2.50
│
└── Private Subnet AZ-2
       │
       └── Endpoint ENI
              10.0.3.50

Your EC2 connects to:

10.0.2.50

instead of reaching the service through the public internet.

5. Real-time DevOps Example

Imagine your company has built an internal:

Central Logging Service

Multiple AWS accounts need to send logs to it.

Architecture:

             LOGGING ACCOUNT
             ────────────────

             Logging Service
                    |
             Network Load Balancer
                    |
             Endpoint Service
                    |
              AWS PrivateLink
                    |
        ┌───────────┼───────────┐
        │           │           │
        ↓           ↓           ↓
      VPC-A        VPC-B       VPC-C
        |           |           |
      EC2          EKS         EC2

Now all three VPCs can privately consume the logging service.

They don't need:

Internet Gateway
NAT Gateway
Public IP

for this service connection.
